WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.272
A human-machine interface, or HMI, is the operator's window into an industrial process.

00:00:05.272 --> 00:00:10.841
It may be a panel mounted near equipment, a workstation in a control room,

00:00:10.841 --> 00:00:13.626
or part of a larger supervisory platform.

00:00:13.626 --> 00:00:17.604
The HMI translates controller data into graphics, values, trends, alarms,

00:00:17.604 --> 00:00:19.991
and controls that people can use.

00:00:19.991 --> 00:00:22.775
Process visibility must be timely and trustworthy.

00:00:22.775 --> 00:00:26.753
Operators rely on displays to understand equipment state, production conditions,

00:00:26.753 --> 00:00:27.947
and developing problems.

00:00:27.947 --> 00:00:31.527
Alarm functions call attention to conditions that require evaluation,

00:00:31.527 --> 00:00:35.108
while acknowledgment and history help teams manage the response.

00:00:35.108 --> 00:00:39.086
Many HMIs also permit manual commands, setpoint changes, mode selection,

00:00:39.086 --> 00:00:40.677
and other authorized actions.

00:00:40.677 --> 00:00:44.257
This makes an HMI more than a passive display.

00:00:44.257 --> 00:00:45.848
Its permissions, application configuration,

00:00:45.848 --> 00:00:48.633
and communication paths can directly influence operations.

00:00:48.633 --> 00:00:51.418
Compromise or misuse can have several effects.

00:00:51.418 --> 00:00:54.998
False or stale information may conceal a real problem.

00:00:54.998 --> 00:00:57.385
An inappropriate command may alter equipment.

00:00:57.385 --> 00:01:03.352
Loss of the interface can leave operators with limited visibility even if controllers continue running.

00:01:03.352 --> 00:01:07.728
A flood of meaningless alarms can distract from conditions that matter.

00:01:07.728 --> 00:01:11.706
Protection includes managed user accounts, least privilege, controlled remote access,

00:01:11.706 --> 00:01:15.287
application and configuration change control, tested updates, secure backups,

00:01:15.287 --> 00:01:16.480
and audit records.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:21.652
HMI systems should not be used for general browsing or routine office work.

00:01:21.652 --> 00:01:26.823
Redundant consoles and manual operating procedures may support resilience when a workstation fails.

00:01:26.823 --> 00:01:30.801
During investigation, distinguish the displayed view from the underlying process.

00:01:30.801 --> 00:01:34.779
Compare HMI evidence with controller status, historian trends, field observations,

00:01:34.779 --> 00:01:35.973
and operator reports.

00:01:35.973 --> 00:01:41.940
The best security response restores trustworthy situational awareness without issuing uncoordinated actions to the process.
